/**
 * Cloudflare Workers — static assets only.
 *
 * WHY NOT vinext / OpenNext. Cloudflare's default guidance for Next.js is
 * vinext, which reimplements the Next runtime on Workers so server rendering
 * works. This app has no server rendering to port: `next build` reports all 76
 * routes as static or SSG, with no dynamic route anywhere. It is a prerendered
 * docs site plus a folder of registry JSON, so it deploys as files — which is
 * fewer moving parts, no framework shim, and nothing to keep in sync with
 * Next's release cadence. If a route ever genuinely needs a server, that is the
 * moment to revisit vinext, and this comment is the trigger.
 *
 * `not_found_handling: "404-page"` serves the exported 404.html for unknown
 * paths. `dynamicParams = false` on both dynamic routes means anything not
 * prerendered is genuinely not ours.
 */
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "echo-ui",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-11",
  /*
   * One canonical URL. With these unset, Cloudflare also serves the site at
   * the account's own workers.dev address and at a preview URL per deployment —
   * three public addresses for one site, which splits search ranking and, worse
   * for a registry, gives people a second host to paste into their
   * `components.json`. An install pinned to a workers.dev URL keeps working
   * right up until it doesn't.
   */
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,

  /*
   * Declared here, not just clicked in the dashboard. Adding the custom domain
   * through the UI works, but it leaves the binding as account state that no
   * checkout of this repo knows about — `wrangler deploy` reported "No targets
   * deployed" precisely because the config described no route. With this, the
   * domain is reproducible: a fresh clone deploys to the same place.
   */
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "echo-ui.com", "custom_domain": true }],

  /*
   * ONE DEPLOY SHIPS THE DOCS AND THE REGISTRY, AND WHEN TO SPLIT THEM.
   *
   * `./out` holds the marketing site, the guides, AND `/e/` — the contract
   * `npx echo-ui-cli` installs from, the one carrying "once a URL is public,
   * it stays". So a broken MDX page is a registry outage, and a copy tweak and
   * a registry publish are the same action. That coupling is accepted
   * deliberately: splitting means a second deploy target, a
   * second set of headers and a second thing to keep in sync, run by one
   * person, and the cost is theoretical until someone depends on `/e/`.
   *
   * It is accepted WITH mitigations, which live in `scripts/prune-fixtures.mjs`
   * (Playwright's fixture routes never reach this directory, and an export with
   * no usable `e/index.json` is refused), in `scripts/released-only.mjs` (a
   * deploy with a component at a version no release froze is refused: the
   * docs can't publish registry files a release hasn't) and in the
   * post-deploy smoke check.
   *
   * SPLIT WHEN EITHER IS TRUE. Pinned here rather than left to memory, in the
   * style of the vinext note above:
   *
   *   1. A docs change needs to ship on a different cadence than a registry
   *      change — someone waits on a copy edit to publish a component, or the
   *      reverse.
   *   2. A docs build breaks the registry ONCE. Not "could": the first time
   *      `/e/` is unreachable because of something on the marketing side, the
   *      cost stopped being theoretical and this note has been paid for.
   *
   * Splitting means a second Worker serving `public/e` with its own
   * `_headers`, and `--registry` still pointing at whatever URL it answers on.
   * Nothing in the `/e/` format assumes one host, which is what keeps this
   * reversible.
   */
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./out",
    "not_found_handling": "404-page"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  }
}
